  "query_preview": "..(Saturday through Thursday) \nIssued at 418 PM EST THU NOV 5 2026\n\nThe start of the forecast period is characterized by quiet weather \nand below average temperatures. Steady, deep northwesterly flow in \nthe wake of Friday's cold front, a building surface high pressure \nsystem, and dry air throughout the column will keep  meaningful \nchances of rain out of the forecast through at least the weekend. \nThe most notable sensible weather impacts this weekend will come \nfrom potential frost and/or river valley fog at night. After any \nremnant fog/frost burn off on Saturday morning, temperatures will \nlikely struggle to warm much higher than the mid/upper 50s. This will \ngive Saturday night's lows a head start in the race towards \nfreezing, but just how cold it gets will be contingent upon how much \ndry air mixes down to the surface earlier this afternoon. A similar \nsetup is in store for Sunday, so mesoscale dewpoint trends will need \nto be monitored closely as the time approaches. Frost is certainly \non the table in the sheltered and shaded hollows on both Saturday \nand Sunday nights, but the geographical extent of the frost outside \nof this is contingent upon how the crossover temperatures will set \nup. If dewpoints are only able to mix down into the mid 30s, fog \ncould be favored over frost. On the other hand, if dewpoints mix to \nvalues well below that critical 32 degrees mark, a widespread frost \n(and potentially even a localized freeze) is plausible. Near the \nmain stem river valleys, fog is currently the favored solution, \nespecially given the recent rainfall. The last frost/freeze forecast \ndid not fully come to fruition, and the baseline NBM model guidance \nused to populate the grids appears to be quite cold in this time \nframe. Thus, agricultural interests are strongly encouraged to \nmonitor for future forecast updates.  \n\nA gradual warming trend kicks off on next week, with temps creeping \ncloser to 70 by midweek. The persistence of a broad surface high \npressure and a return to quasi-zonal flow aloft should also keep us \ndry through the end of the period, although there is a signal that \nthe upper level pattern could turn more active in the latter half of \nthe work week. Rain chances accordingly creep back into the forecast \nfrom Wednesday night into Thursday, but those PoPs are quite low at \nthis time. This is a testament to lingering model spread regarding \nthe setup, so those with late week outdoor plans should monitor to \nsee if those rain chances increase with subsequent forecast \npackages.",
